{"filename": "PalladiumQ12007.md", "category": "Материалы конференций", "num_pages": 12, "char_count": 33819, "likely_scanned": false, "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Precious Metals Quarterly 1Q2007      Palladium \n\n# Palladium \n\n## Recent trends \n\nPalladium broke out of its trading range in a technically significant manner at the end of 2006, finally reaching $340/oz and then $350/oz by the end of January on the back of fresh fund buying and strong gains in platinum.  Like its sister metal, palladium too succumbed to the weakness in the equity markets in early March but (perhaps surprisingly) from a technical standpoint the weakness in this market was a lot less than had been registered in other PGMs. \n\n- Industrial end-users have been sidelined with palladium’s gradual move upwards and are waiting for a bigger correction than that which occurred in early March. \n\n- Despite palladium’s rather poor fundamental outlook, speculators have continued to take up long positions on NYMEX.  The net non-commercial long position increased by more than 5,100 contracts (16 tonnes) since the end of 2006 to a high of 7,020 contracts (21.8 tonnes) on 13 March.  Though this position has since reduced by some 300 contracts, it is likely that the conspicuous lack of volatility has had a bearing on investor attitudes towards this metal, being an attractive quality to those looking for a long-term bull strategy. \n\n- Bullion imports of palladium into Zurich increased by 675% year-on-year in December 2006.  While large imports of palladium from Russia into Switzerland at the beginning or end of the calendar year is not in itself an unusual occurrence, the scale of the increase last December served to underline the metal’s fundamental situation and temporarily inhibited it’s movement higher in line with the rest of the precious metals complex in February. \n\n- Total Russian shipments for the full year 2006 amounted to 5,026,172oz, up 36.5% compared with 2005.  This reflects the higher quantities of metal being exported to Switzerland and Hong Kong - trade data shows that, year-on-year, imports from Russia increased by 257% and 107% respectively.  Output at Norilsk increased by 31,000oz to 3,164,000oz in 2006. \n\n- North American Palladium produced 73,242oz during 2006 Q4, up 99% compared with the corresponding period of 2005 due to improved mill feed grades and metal recovery.  Full year output for 2006 therefore increased by 34% to 237,388oz. \n\nCRU International Limited confidential \n\n52 \n\nPrecious Metals Quarterly 1Q2007    Palladium \n\n### Palladium: conspicuous lack of volatility \n\nPd was much less volatile compared with Pt during 2006... \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nVolatility (SD)<br>250<br>200<br>150<br>100<br>50<br>0<br>Platinum Palladium<br>1996 1997 1998 1999 2000 2001 2002 2003 2004 2005 2006<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### …an attractive characteristic for \n\ninvestors amid recent nervousness in the equity and commodities markets... \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nNo of contracts US$/oz<br>12000<br>8000<br>4000<br>0<br>Jan-03 Dec-03 Dec-04 Dec-05 Dec-06<br>-4000<br>-8000<br>Non-comm longs Non-comm shorts<br>Ne"}, {"page": 2, "text": "t Non-comm pos (lhs)<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### … this helped the Pd price make substantial gains on 2005 levels... \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nNo. of occurrences<br>180<br>160 2005<br>140<br>120<br>100 2004<br>2006<br>80<br>60 2002<br>2003<br>40<br>20<br>0<br>100 150 200 250 300 350 400 450<br>Pd price resilient given level of selling on<br>TOCOM<br>No. of contracts OI Pd price in yen<br>25,000 1,400<br>1,200<br>20,000<br>1,000<br>15,000 800<br>600<br>10,000<br>400<br>5,000<br>200<br>0 0<br>TOCOM OI Pd price in Yen<br>Jan-05Apr-05Jul-05Oct-05Jan-06Apr-06 Jul-06Oct-06Jan-07<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### … and increase its discount over Pt to \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nmore than $800 again in 2007<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n$/oz $/oz<br>1,400 600<br>1,200 400<br>200<br>1,000<br>0<br>800<br>-200<br>600<br>-400<br>400<br>-600<br>200 -800<br>0 -1000<br>Pd-Pt spread (RHS) Pt price Pd price<br>Jan-98Jan-99Jan-00Jan-01Jan-02Jan-03Jan-04Jan-05Jan-06Jan-07<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### New supplies to keep market in surplus \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n'000  US$/oz<br>3,400 800<br>700<br>2,200<br>600<br>1,000<br>500<br>-200 400<br>300<br>-1,400<br>200<br>-2,600<br>100<br>-3,800 0<br>1990      1994        1998         2002         2006       2010<br>Balance<br>Balance incl stock movements<br>Pd price (rhs)<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\nPrecious Metals Quarterly 1Q2007    Palladium \n\n53 \n\n### Palladium: demand encouraging in the autocat sector \n\n#### Full year data for 2006 shows that \n\n#### Switzerland now accounts for 29% of total \n\n#### Pd imports \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nkgs<br>75,000<br>…and the country exported 35% less<br>60,000 palladium compared with 2005<br>45,000<br>30,000<br>15,000<br>0<br>Jan-01Jul-01Jan-02Jul-02Jan-03Jul-03Jan-04Jul-04Jan-05Jul-05Jan-06Jul-06Jan-07<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### Chinese demand for Pt in autocatalysts strong in 2006 \n\nGlobal bullion imports for 2006 increased by approximately 22% year-on-year (trade data for India is available to August). While Sw itzerland accounted for much of this, modest increases w ere also recorded for Germany, Hong Kong and France. \n\nImports in to Hong Kong increased by 34% year-on-year to 1.37Moz, w hich suggests that manufacturing of palladium jew ellery in China w as robust last year.  How ever, retail orders are likely to have fallen as a result of large inventories built up in 2005.  In January 2007 imports w ere at the low est level since February 2004. \n\nImports into China are a proxy for demand in the autocatalyst sector. Trade data show s that imports of palladium increased by 62% year-on-year in 2006 to 308koz - this is hardly surprising given the huge grow th in the Chinese auto industry last year. \n\n#### Marginal thrifting to continue in Europe and the USA... \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n% of Pd used per autocat<br>1.00<br>…but Pd loadings increasing elsewhere, l"}, {"page": 3, "text": "ifting the<br>global average to 0.45% by 2011<br>0.80<br>0.60<br>0.40<br>0.20<br>0.00<br>Europe USA Japan<br>ROW Global average<br>1990 1992 1994 1996 1998 2000 2002 2004 2006 2008 2010<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### Pd loading in autocats likely to surpass that of Pt... \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nAverage PGM loading on an autocat<br>1.0<br>0.8<br>0.6<br>0.4<br>0.2<br>0.0<br>Platinum Palladium Rhodium<br>1990 1992 1994 1996 1998 2000 2002 2004 2006<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### …given the persistent discount of palladium to platinum \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nUS$/oz<br>700<br>400<br>100<br>-200<br>-500<br>-800<br>-1100<br>Pd premium/discount to Pt<br>Jan-00Aug-00Mar-01Oct-01May-02Dec-02Jul-03Feb-04Sep-04Apr-05Nov-05Jun-06Jan-07<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### Furthermore, global vehicle production is forecast to grow by 13% by 2011 \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n'000 units<br>90,000<br>60,000<br>30,000<br>0<br>Vehicle production Vehicles fitted w ith autocats<br>1990 1992 1994 1996 1998 2000 2002 2004 2006 2008 2010<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\n54 \n\nPrecious Metals Quarterly 1Q2007    Palladium \n\n### Palladium: Russian shipments at five-year high in 2006 \n\nTotal mine supply is forecast to grow by 16% to 8.8Moz in 2011 \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n'000oz<br>6,000<br>5,000<br>4,000<br>3,000<br>2,000<br>1,000<br>0<br>South Africa Zimbabw e Canada<br>USA Other Uncommitted<br>1990 1992 1994 1996 1998 2000 2002 2004 2006 2008 2010<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\nSouth Africa is forecast to overtake Russia as the leading producer of Pd in \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n2008<br>'000oz<br>9,600<br>8,400<br>7,200<br>6,000<br>4,800<br>3,600<br>2,400<br>1,200<br>0<br>South Africa Russia North America Other<br>1990 1992 1994 1996 1998 2000 2002 2004 2006 2008 2010<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### Russian stocks were further depleted in 2006... \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n'000oz<br>31,500<br>27,000<br>22,500<br>18,000<br>13,500<br>9,000<br>4,500<br>0<br>Russia US DLA Stillw ater<br>1990 1992 1994 1996 1998 2000 2002 2004 2006<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### Pd recovered from scrap could account for more than 16% of total supply by \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n2011<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n'000oz %<br>2,000 20<br>16<br>1,500<br>12<br>1,000<br>8<br>500<br>4<br>0 0<br>Scrap recovery % of annual supply<br>19901992199419961998200020022004200620082010<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### …as shipments increased by 249% yearon-year \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n'000oz<br>5,000<br>4,000<br>3,000<br>2,000<br>1,000<br>0<br>Shipments from stock<br>1990 1992 1994 1996 1998 2000 2002 2004 2006<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n#### Supplies heavily reliant on mine output, but no shortage likely \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n'000oz<br>12,000<br>10,000<br>8,000<br>6,000<br>4,000<br>2,000<br>0<br>Committed mine production Uncommitted mine produc"}, {"page": 4, "text": "tion<br>Russian shipments USA DLA Sales<br>Stillw ater stock sales Scrap recovery<br>1990 1992 1994 1996 1998 2000 2002 2004 2006 2008 2010<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\nPrecious Metals Quarterly 1Q2007    Palladium \n\n55 \n\n## Forecast update: investment demand key to maintaining prices \n\nFollowing huge growth in palladium jewellery offtake in China over 2004/05, the recycling of Pd950 stock put a dent in demand last year.  Further growth is now very much dependant on it’s popularity spreading to the primary cities and making an impact in other markets, such as the USA.  The sustained Pt-Pd price spread should help to drive offtake at the fashion end of the spectrum.  We believe that there is potential for substantial growth in this sector with effective marketing initiatives. \n\nThe USA auto market looks to be stagnating with foreign manufacturers pulling the rug from under domestic producers (see _Platinum Forecast Update_ ).  CRU forecasts that autocatalytic demand for palladium in North America will grow by an annual average of less than 3% over the forecast period.  However, the demand outlook for China is very positive given the recent and projected growth in vehicle production.  This will offset weakness in the USA - global demand for palladium in autocats could exceed 5.3Moz by 2011. \n\nTrade data for 2006 highlights the fact that there are still ample stocks of palladium. Shipments from Russia totalled 59.7 tonnes in 2006, which is 249% higher compared with the year before.  The market is still reliant on Russian shipments; however, the country’s stockpile is a mere fraction of what it was a decade ago.  With less scope for large stock sales, this will help rein in the market surplus over the forecast period. \n\nSwitzerland imported 40.1 tonnes of palladium from Russia in December 2006, bringing full year imports from Russia alone to 71.6 tonnes (the highest level since 2001).  Total imports for 2006 amounted to 126.2 tonnes, up 174% on 2005, and with exports down by 35% year-on-year at 48.1 tonnes; implied inventories now stand just below 230 tonnes by our calculation.  Large inventories of palladium will likely prevent any sustained upward trend in prices, especially factoring in growth in primary and secondary supplies. \n\nRecovery of palladium from scrap is set to grow by a substantial rate over the forecast period, and could increase by almost 30% in 2007 alone as a result of the large number of cars built in the mid- to late-nineties (during which time palladium loading in autocatalysts increased, see _Palladium Storyboard_ ) that are now being scrapped.  Furthermore, mine production is forecast to exceed 8.5Moz by 2011. \n\nPalladium outperformed both platinum and gold over 2006, owing to its projected growth in jewellery demand and substitution for platinum in the autocat sector.  Investor interest was a key driver of prices, with market participants being attracted by low volatility but overlooking high inventories and increasing "}, {"page": 5, "text": "supplies.  This means that palladium is vulnerable to changes in sentiment, but with the platinum and gold markets expected to remain strong in the short- to medium-term; we do not expect prices to fall below $300/oz. \n\nCRU forecast’s a long-term average palladium price of $339/oz. \n\n56 \n\nPrecious Metals Quarterly 1Q2007    Palladium \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nLong term average annual palladium prices<br>900<br>800<br>700<br>600<br>500<br>400<br>US$339/oz<br>300<br>200<br>100<br>0<br>1965 1970 1975 1980 1985 1990 1995 2000 2005<br>Pd price (2006US$/oz)<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\nPrecious Metals Quarterly 1Q2007    Palladium \n\n57 \n\n|Table 1: Pall|adium prices|(US$/oz), 198<br>Average|7-2011<br>Low|High|Range|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|**Selected Period**|**Averages:**|||||\n|1987-2006||231|81|1,090|1,009|\n|**2006-2011**||**335**|**250**|**404**|**154**|\n|**Annual:**||||||\n||1987|130|111|145|33|\n||1988|123|119|131|12|\n||1989|144|134|166|33|\n||1990|114|90|135|46|\n||1991|90|82|97|15|\n||1992|85|81|107|26|\n||1993|115|107|140|33|\n||1994|136|124|156|32|\n||1995|156|132|169|38|\n||1996|128|117|139|22|\n||1997|178|121|212|90|\n||1998|285|201|417|216|\n||1999|359|285|454|169|\n||2000|683|433|970|537|\n||2001<br>|603<br>|315<br>|1090<br>|775<br>|\n||2002|337|248|440|192|\n||2003|200|148|269|121|\n||2004|230|178|333|155|\n||2005|202|170|220|50|\n||2006|320|253|404|151|\n||2007|343|300|380|80|\n||2008|330|250|350|100|\n||2009|340|260|380|120|\n||2010|340|260|380|120|\n||2011|340|260|